1. Piper text-to-speech (used as a local tool; not included here)

   The lesson audio is rendered with piper-tts 1.7.0 (https://github.com/OHF-Voice/piper1-gpl),
   licensed GPL-3.0-or-later. (The original rhasspy/piper, versions up to 1.2, is MIT; the
   build used here is the newer GPL one.) pipeline/tts.py runs it in a separate process;
   pipeline/piper_worker.py runs inside Piper's own Python environment and imports the
   `piper` module. That adapter is separately licensed under GPL-3.0-or-later, matching the tool it imports;
   see its SPDX header and LICENSES/GPL-3.0.txt. Piper itself is not distributed here.

2. Narrator voice model and its training data   (chosen 2026-10-02; details in docs/VOICE.md)

   Voice: en_US-john-medium ("John", US English male) by Bryce Beattie, from
   https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices (en/en_US/john/medium) and
   https://brycebeattie.com/files/tts/
   Model licence: public domain per its MODEL_CARD and the author's page ("Feel free to use
   these for any legal and ethical purpose"); the piper-voices repository is MIT.
   Dataset: about 12.5 hours of LibriVox recordings (https://librivox.org), fine-tuned from the
   author's "Kristin" voice, itself trained from scratch on LibriVox. Dataset licence: public domain.
   This applies to audio rendered with that voice (~/Downloads/subject-mastery-build/audio-en_US-john-medium).
   Audio rendered earlier with en_US-lessac-medium (research-only Blizzard 2013 data) must not
   be published.

3. Bible translations quoted in lessons (public domain)

   - King James Version (1611/1769). Public domain, except in the United Kingdom, where the
     Crown's letters patent apply.
   - World English Bible (WEB). Public domain (dedicated by its publisher, eBible.org).
   - American Standard Version (1901). Public domain.
   Lessons name the translation whenever they quote; pipeline/check_quotes.py checks every
   quotation word for word against these texts.

